 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Occlusions/Low-Texture/Non-Lambertian:** Multi-view depth estimation fails in these areas. DepthSplat mitigates this by augmenting multi-view cost volumes with monocular features [S1].
-*   **Scale Ambiguity:** Monocular depth estimation suffers from scale ambiguities across views. DepthSplat maintains multi-view consistency by using monocular features only as an augmentation to multi-view matching, not as the primary depth source [S1].
-*   **Error Propagation:** Unlike methods that refine coarse depth with monocular features (e.g., TranSplat), DepthSplat combines cost volume and monocular features early to avoid error propagation from coarse stages [S1].
+*   **Occlusions, Low-Texture, Non-Lambertian Surfaces:** Multi-view depth estimation fails in these scenarios due to violated photometric consistency assumptions [S1]. DepthSplat mitigates this by augmenting multi-view cost volumes with monocular features [S1].
+*   **Scale Ambiguity:** Monocular depth estimation suffers from scale ambiguities across multiple views [S1]. DepthSplat maintains multi-view consistency by using monocular features only as an augmentation to multi-view matching, not as the primary depth source [S1].
+*   **Error Propagation:** Unlike methods that refine coarse depth with monocular features (e.g., TranSplat), DepthSplat combines cost volumes and monocular features early to avoid error propagation from coarse stages [S1].
+*   **Computational Cost:** For $N > 2$ views, computing correlations for all pairs is expensive. DepthSplat selects only the top-2 nearest neighbors to maintain tractability and scale efficiently [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gaussian Graph Network](18863-gaussian-graph-network.md)
-A feed-forward method that generates efficient and generalizable 3D Gaussian representations by modeling relations between Gaussian groups from different views using a Gaussian Graph structure. It solves artifacts and excessive memory costs from simply combining pixel-aligned Gaussians. Unlike DepthSplat which focuses on robust depth prediction via feature matching, GGN focuses on inter-view Gaussian relations. Choose GGN when inter-view consistency of Gaussian primitives is critical beyond depth accuracy.
+A feed-forward method that generates efficient and generalizable 3D Gaussian representations from multi-view images by modeling relations between Gaussian groups from different views using a Gaussian Graph structure. It solves the problem of artifacts and excessive memory costs caused by previous methods that simply combine pixel-aligned Gaussians from multiple views without capturing their inter-view relations. Unlike sibling methods that treat multi-view Gaussians as independent sets, GGN performs message passing and aggregation at the Gaussian level, allowing each Gaussian to benefit from connected groups in other views.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Reconstruction speed is 0.6 seconds for feed-forward reconstruction from 12 input views at 512x960 resolution [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** State-of-the-art performance on ScanNet, RealEstate10K, and DL3DV datasets [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Computation scales efficiently to larger numbers of input views by selecting only the top-2 nearest neighbors for cost volume construction [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Robust to occlusions, low-texture, and non-Lambertian surfaces; maintains multi-view consistency; cheaper to train (4 GPUs vs 64 A100s for GS-LRM) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on pre-trained monocular depth features; multi-view transformer adds computational overhead compared to pure CNN-based methods [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When robust feed-forward 3D Gaussian reconstruction from multi-view images is needed, especially in challenging scenes with low texture or reflections.
-*   **When not to use:** When per-scene optimization is acceptable and higher quality is prioritized over speed; when computational resources are extremely limited and pre-trained models cannot be used.
+*   **Reconstruction Speed:** 0.6 seconds for feed-forward reconstruction from 12 input views at 512x960 resolution [S1].
+*   **Training Cost:** Trained in 2 days with 4 GPUs, compared to methods like GS-LRM which require 64 A100 GPUs for 2 days [S1].
+*   **Scalability:** Scales efficiently to a larger number of input views by selecting only the top-2 nearest neighbors for cost volume construction [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Robust depth prediction in challenging scenarios (low-texture, occlusions) by leveraging monocular features; state-of-the-art performance on ScanNet, RealEstate10K, and DL3DV datasets [S1]. Enables unsupervised pre-training of depth models using Gaussian splatting as a pre-training objective [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the pre-trained monocular depth model; performance may degrade if the monocular features are inaccurate or misaligned [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When fast, feed-forward reconstruction from sparse multi-view images is required, especially in challenging environments where traditional multi-view stereo fails.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When ground truth depth is available and per-scene optimization is feasible, or when computational resources are extremely limited and pre-trained models cannot be loaded.
 
 ## Background
-DepthSplat was introduced to connect Gaussian splatting and depth estimation, studying their interactions [S1]. It was developed to overcome the individual limitations of multi-view stereo (failure in occlusions/low-texture) and monocular depth estimation (scale ambiguity) [S1]. The method was trained on TartanAir, ScanNet, RealEstate10K, and DL3DV datasets [S1]. It also demonstrates that Gaussian splatting can serve as an unsupervised pre-training objective for learning powerful depth models from large-scale multi-view posed datasets [S1].
